ight  expect you might think that such  protracted solitude would lead to an  introspection to grow in self-awareness  but not for me what happened was that I  began to forget myself my focus shifted  almost entirely outwards  It was as if we gained our sense of self  from our interaction with other people  alone there was no need for identity for  self definition so actually going deeper  and deeper into the personal self  becoming more of an I actually you lose  contact with you I bet  actually the self and our sense of self  is actually incredibly fluid and one of  the reasons for that is that the self  our sense of self is actually a category  we can think of ourselves as individuals  I Alex or I can think of myself as a  group member and I can think of myself  as a member of different groups I can  say I'm British or I can say I'm a  social scientist or a male so the  question is when do particular  identities become salient again you  might think that's a simple question  actually it's peculiarly complicated in  if I were to answer in a single sentence  the answer is to when we define  ourselves in terms of a particular  identity is when it makes sense to do so  you're more likely to define yourself as  Canadian if you're watching Canada play  sports and the Olympics that comparative  context makes that identity salient if  the sport in some sense of activity is  typical of the identity so you're more  likely to feel Canadian if you're  watching ice hockey even if you're  watching a field hockey program you're  watching tennis and also if being  Canadian has meant something to you in  the class then we tend to see ourselves  as similar to other members of that  category of that group so if you had all  just if you happen to bump into each  other under on the metro on the way here  you wouldn't have felt you had any in  common with the person sitting next to  you but now that you're in this room and  your relationship to that other person  is defined by the sha